--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PREREQUISITES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
An AArch64 cross-compiler (aarch64-none-elf), CMake, Just, mtools, and cpio.
QEMU (qemu-system-aarch64) for running. See README.txt for the toolchain file.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE LOOP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. Branch off dev (never off main, never commit to main directly):
       git checkout dev && git pull origin dev
       git checkout -b feature/short-descriptive-name

  2. Build and iterate:
       just build            # Debug by default; `just build release` for opt
       just run              # headless in QEMU
       just run-gui          # windowed
       just debug / just gdb # start under GDB and attach

  3. Match the house style (docs/coding_style_guidelines.txt). Before you push:
       just format           # apply clang-format
       just check-format     # verify it's clean (this is enforced)

  4. Test — see docs/testing.txt:
       just test             # builds the CONFIG_TESTS kernel and runs the
                             # in-kernel suites headless; must exit 0

  5. Push and open a PR INTO dev (not main):
       git push origin feature/short-descriptive-name
     Keep the PR focused; explain the "why," not just the "what."

  6. After review and merge, delete the feature branch.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GETTING TO A RELEASE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Promotion is dev -> main, and main is meant to be flashable at any moment. The
full rules (hotfixes, tags, branch protection) are in docs/branch_workflow.txt.
The two gates that are easy to forget:

  - `just test` must pass in QEMU, AND
  - dev must be validated ON REAL HARDWARE before it is merged to main.

QEMU-green alone is not enough to promote — see docs/testing.txt.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
COMMIT MESSAGES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Use a type prefix consistent with the existing history, e.g.:
  feat:      a new capability
  fix:       a bug fix (name the subsystem: `fix(fat32): ...`)
  refactor:  behavior-preserving restructuring
  docs:      documentation only
  test:      tests only
  build:     build system / config

Write the subject in the imperative mood and keep the body focused on intent.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A FEW GROUND RULES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - uapi/ is the user/kernel ABI. Changing a syscall number or a shared struct
    layout affects both sides at once — do it deliberately.
  - Never trust a user pointer in the kernel; go through the uaccess helpers.
  - Correctness of the foundation beats breadth of features. Finish and test a
    layer before building the next one on top of it.
